Q: unset is not working in a for loop: data does not change A: <p>The problem is that unsetting an array element reduces the value that <code>count()</code> returns, so your loop ends prematurely. You shouldn't use <code>count()</code> during the loop, get the value before the loop starts.</p>
<pre><code>$playerCount = count($playersPool);
for ($i = 0; $i &lt; $playerCount; $i++) {
   if(in_array($playersPool[$r]['id'], $excludedPlayers)){
       Schema::writeToLog('player removed: '.$playersPool[$r]['id']);
       unset($playersPool[$r]);
   }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Also, after doing all these unsets, you should reset the indexes with</p>
<pre><code>$playersPool = array_values($playersPool);
</code></pre>
<p>Another option would be to use <code>array_filter()</code> to remove elements.</p>
<pre><code>$playersPool = array_filter($playersPool, function($player) {
    if(in_array($playersPool[$r]['id'], $excludedPlayers)){
        Schema::writeToLog('player removed: '.$playersPool[$r]['id']);
        return false;
    } else {
        return true;
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p>This solves both problems.</p>

Q: How to programmatically detect whether a font renders characters as squares? A: <p>Your loop processes three fonts in each iteration, to display them in three columns. But you are only checking one font in each iteration, the font shown in the middle column. So ⅔ of the fonts are not checked at all.</p>
<p>This demonstrates why code should not serve different concerns. Such expensive operations shouldn’t be (repeatedly) done in a painting method anyway. But checking each font becomes much simpler when done in a simple loop rather than intertwined with a three-column rendering operation, especially when the outcome of the check doesn’t affect the rendering at all, as you are rendering all font names anyway, regardless of the check’s outcome.</p>
<p>Besides that, the code</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>int ind= theFont.canDisplayUpTo(&quot;zÀÿĀ&quot;);
if (ind&gt;0 &amp;&amp; ind&lt;3) { // 'z' is recognized.
</code></pre>
<p>doesn’t match what <code>canDisplayUpTo</code> does. When the font can display all of the four specified letters, it will return <code>-1</code>. So, when the first letter <code>z</code> is recognized, the result may be <code>-1</code> or a value <code>&gt;0</code>. The result can never be larger than the index of the last character, so the <code>ind&lt;3</code> test is obsolete.</p>
<p>As side notes, the <code>ScrollBarListener</code> is obsolete. A <code>JScrollPane</code> takes care of repainting its content on any change of its scrollbars. Further, the reason why a non-static <code>main</code> method causes the UI to open twice in your code, is that the launcher has to create an instance of your class, to invoke a non-static <code>main</code> method but your UI initialization code is inside the constructor, so it is already executed one time before your main method in this scenario. The solution is not performing such operations inside the constructor, in fact, there is no reason to create a subclass of <code>JFrame</code> at all.</p>
<p>When addressing all these issues and separating the font processing code from the painting code, it’s a good opportunity to use built-in classes for the rendering, which simplifies the code to</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import java.awt.*;
import javax.swing.*;

public class FontsMany {
    record FontInfo(Font font, boolean canDisplayName) {}

    static void showFonts() {
        JFrame frame = new JFrame();
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(WindowConstants.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        frame.setSize(640, 700);
        String[] names = GraphicsEnvironment.getLocalGraphicsEnvironment()
                                            .getAvailableFontFamilyNames();
        frame.setTitle(names.length + &quot; installierte Fonts&quot;);
        FontInfo[] infos = new FontInfo[names.length];
        for(int ix = 0; ix &lt; names.length; ix++) {
            Font font = new Font(names[ix], Font.PLAIN, 11);
            infos[ix] = new FontInfo(font, font.canDisplayUpTo(font.getName()) &lt; 0);
        }
        JList&lt;FontInfo&gt; list = new JList&lt;&gt;(infos);
        list.setBackground(Color.WHITE);
        Font fallbackFont = new Font(Font.DIALOG, 0, 11);
        ListCellRenderer&lt;Object&gt; r = new DefaultListCellRenderer();
        list.setCellRenderer((l, value, index, isSelected, cellFocus) -&gt; {
            Component c = r.getListCellRendererComponent(
                              l, value.font.getName(), index, isSelected, cellFocus);
            c.setFont(value.canDisplayName? value.font: fallbackFont);
            c.setForeground(value.canDisplayName? Color.BLACK: Color.LIGHT_GRAY);
            return c;
        });
        list.setFixedCellWidth(200);
        list.setLayoutOrientation(JList.VERTICAL_WRAP);
        list.setVisibleRowCount((names.length + 2) / 3);
        frame.setContentPane(new JScrollPane(list));
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        EventQueue.invokeLater(FontsMany::showFonts);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This example renders the fonts that can not render their own name with a fall-back font and gray color, so they are easily recognized. You can also use the <code>FontInfo[]</code> array for further processing in your application.</p>

Q: Merging the headers then concatenate the records of a huge number of text files A: <p>Regarding the second problem - <code>Argument list too long</code> is a shell error message, not an awk one, and the usual solution is to have awk read the list of files from the input rather than passing them as arguments, e.g. using the builtin version of <code>printf</code> as it can't have that argument list size problem:</p>
<pre><code>printf '%s\n' input*.vcf |
awk '
    BEGIN {
        while ( (getline file &lt; ARGV[1]) &gt; 0 ) {
            ARGV[ARGC++] = file
        }
    }
    ...
' -
</code></pre>
<p>Given the OPs updated sample input/output and using any awk you could do:</p>
<pre><code>$ cat tst.sh
#!/usr/bin/env bash

awk '
    BEGIN {
        while ( (getline file &lt; ARGV[1]) &gt; 0 ) {
            ARGV[ARGC++] = file
        }
        ARGV[1] = &quot;pass=1&quot;
        numFiles = ARGC-1
        ARGV[ARGC++] = &quot;pass=2&quot;
        for ( i=2; i&lt;=numFiles; i++ ) {
            ARGV[ARGC++] = ARGV[i]
        }
    }
    pass == 1 {
        if ( /^#/ ) {
            if ( /^##contig=/ ) {
                contigs = contigs $0 ORS
            }
        }
        else {
            nextfile
        }
        next
    }
    pass == 2 {
        if ( /^#/ ) {
            if ( !doneHdrs ) {
                if ( /^##contig=/ ) {
                    printf &quot;%s&quot;, contigs
                    contigs = &quot;&quot;
                }
                else {
                    print
                }
            }
        }
        else {
            doneHdrs = 1
            print
        }
    }
' &lt;(printf '%s\n' file*.vcf)
</code></pre>
<p>
<pre><code>$ ./tst.sh
##fileformat=VCFv4.2
##contig=&lt;ID=CL1727,...&gt;
##contig=&lt;ID=CL1903,...&gt;
##contig=&lt;ID=CL2254,...&gt;
##INFO=&lt;ID=AO,...&gt;
##INFO=&lt;ID=RO,...&gt;
##INFO=...
##FORMAT=&lt;ID=GT,...&gt;
##FORMAT=&lt;ID=GQ,...&gt;
##FORMAT=...
#CHROM  POS  ...
CL1727  1362 ...
CL1727  1369 ...
CL1903  892  ...
CL2254  1092 ...
CL2254  2356 ...
</code></pre>
<p>In reality, though, I'd probably just use <code>awk</code> (e.g. using any POSIX-compliant awk below for <code>nextfile</code>) to produce the header block and then <code>grep</code> to print the rest:</p>
<pre><code>$ cat tst.sh
#!/usr/bin/env bash

awk '
    BEGIN {
        while ( (getline file &lt; ARGV[1]) &gt; 0 ) {
            ARGV[ARGC++] = file
        }
        ARGV[1] = &quot;&quot;
        FS = &quot;=&quot;
    }
    /^[^#]/ { nextfile }
    ($1 == &quot;##contig&quot;) || !seen[$0]++ {
        if ( !($1 in vals) ) {
            keys[++numKeys] = $1
        }
        vals[$1] = vals[$1] $0 ORS
    }
    END {
        for ( keyNr=1; keyNr&lt;=numKeys; keyNr++ ) {
            key = keys[keyNr]
            printf &quot;%s&quot;, vals[key]
        }
    }
' &lt;(printf '%s\n' file*.vcf)

xargs grep -hv '^#' &lt; &lt;(printf '%s\n' file*.vcf)
</code></pre>
<p>
<pre><code>$ ./tst.sh
##fileformat=VCFv4.2
##contig=&lt;ID=CL1727,...&gt;
##contig=&lt;ID=CL1903,...&gt;
##contig=&lt;ID=CL2254,...&gt;
##INFO=&lt;ID=AO,...&gt;
##INFO=&lt;ID=RO,...&gt;
##INFO=...
##FORMAT=&lt;ID=GT,...&gt;
##FORMAT=&lt;ID=GQ,...&gt;
##FORMAT=...
#CHROM  POS  ...
CL1727  1362 ...
CL1727  1369 ...
CL1903  892  ...
CL2254  1092 ...
CL2254  2356 ...
</code></pre>
<p>That <code>grep</code> uses the non-standard <code>-h</code> option to suppress printing of file names. If your grep doesn't support <code>-h</code> you could do either of these or similar:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>xargs sed '/^#/d' &lt; &lt;(printf '%s\n' file*.vcf)</code></li>
<li><code>xargs cat &lt; &lt;(printf '%s\n' file*.vcf) | grep -v '^#'</code></li>
</ul>

Q: Formatting seconds in std::chrono A: <p>What you did is akin to:</p>
<pre><code>void please_print( int x ) { printf(&quot;%d&quot;, x); }
void please_print( double x ) { printf(&quot;%lf&quot;, x); }

double pi = 3.14159;
pi = (int)pi;
please_print(pi);
</code></pre>
<p>vs</p>
<pre><code>double pi = 3.14159;
int int_pi = (int)pi;
please_print(int_pi);
</code></pre>
<p>In the first case you have a <code>double</code> which is assigned the value of an int and printed.  In the second case we have an int that is printed.</p>
<p>The overloaded <code>please_print</code> reflects the act that <code>std::format</code> is type-aware; it knows what type of variable it is passed.  Specifically</p>
<pre><code>std::format( &quot;{0:%H:%M:%S}&quot;, time_point )
</code></pre>
<p>knows what type <code>time_point</code> is.  So when it prints, it uses that information.</p>
<p><code>time_point</code>s store their precision in their type, not as a value.  a <code>time_point&lt;seconds&gt;</code> and <code>time_point&lt;milliseconds&gt;</code> are different types.  You can assign variables of different <code>time_point</code>s to each other; the language injects code that does the conversion for you.</p>
<p>This is akin to how you can assign an <code>int</code> to a <code>double</code>.  Doing so doesn't change the <code>double</code> into an <code>int</code>, it just converts the <code>int</code> to the <code>double</code>.</p>
<p>At any later time, you just have the value and the type.  You don't have the entire history of the variable.</p>
<p>For people coming from languages with a looser type system, they expect variables to carry the type of the value along with them.  This can be confusing.  You can implement this in C++ (<code>std::any</code> and similar), but it isn't by default how the language works.</p>
